  [34mdescription:[0m  Operator request: replace the fragile direct,recorded model with a simpler, safer reintegration contract that behaves like normal git collaboration and has zero risk of mutating an agent's own checkout during reintegration.\n\nProblem / evidence:\n- bd-95cda5 and follow-up recurrence reports show direct,recorded can return bd-1d514b while refs/state have already moved.\n- Reported examples include fork/main and/or origin/main advancing to synthetic commits, cacophony-state summaries being published, and local agent HEAD not being an ancestor after retry.\n- This is confusing because regular git collaboration should be: try merge/squash, succeed or reject with conflict/stale-base, and leave the agent branch recoverable.\n- The current recorded path appears to mix code landing, summary/state publication, lifecycle bookkeeping, and checkout sync/repair into one operation, so partial side effects can make the final outcome ambiguous.\n\nDesired product contract:\n1. caco agent reintegrate must treat the agent checkout as an immutable input. It must not implicitly rebase, merge, reset, sync, checkout another branch, rewrite the agent branch, or otherwise mutate the agent working tree.\n2. Any operation that mutates the agent checkout must be explicit and separately named, e.g. caco agent rebase. Reintegration may reject with stale_base and tell the agent to rebase, but must not auto-rebase.\n3. Before any publish attempt, reintegration must create/confirm a recoverable backup ref for the agent HEAD, e.g. refs/caco-backup/<agent-id>/<attempt-id>/head or an equivalent local backup branch/ref. The receipt must print it.\n4. Reintegration should run merge/squash preparation in an isolated integration worktree/temp checkout, not in the agent checkout.\n5. Publish to main must use an explicit compare-and-swap/lease model against the expected target SHA, ideally serialized by a per-project/per-target-branch integration lock or merge queue. If the target moved, reject cleanly; do not mutate agent checkout.\n6. Reintegration results must be transactional from the agent perspective and return a structured receipt. Possible outcomes should be at least: accepted, rejected_no_publish, accepted_with_warning / accepted_state_pending. If any remote ref moved, the command must detect that and report which ref moved instead of returning an opaque failure.\n7. Plain direct mode should be the default safe path. It should behave as a normal branch landing/squash only.\n8. For direct mode, agent summaries/artifacts should be normal repo-committed files on the agent branch, e.g. .cacophony/agent/<agent-id>/summary/<n>/summary.md or a documented successor layout. They land with the code commit/squash on main. No separate cacophony-state publication should be required for direct mode.\n9. direct,recorded should be disabled/deprecated as a default path until redesigned. If retained, recorded state publication must be separated from code landing and must not cause a code landing to be reported as failure after the code ref moved. Rejected attempts may publish attempt logs, but must not masquerade as landed summaries.\n10. Profiles/docs should be updated so agents use direct mode plus repo-committed summaries by default, and only use recorded/PR flows when explicitly requested.\n\nImplementation guidance / likely work areas:\n- Inspect caco agent reintegrate implementation in caco-daemon/caco-cli and profile/lifecycle hooks.\n- Find all places where reintegrate mutates/syncs the agent checkout or performs auto-rebase/auto-merge; remove from reintegrate or gate behind explicit separate commands.\n- Add backup-ref creation before publish attempts.\n- Add/extend receipt JSON and human output with attempt_id, agent_head, backup_ref, target_before, target_after, landed_commit, state_before/state_after if any, checkout_mutated=false, and published refs.\n- Add post-error verification: after any publish error, fetch/inspect relevant refs and report accepted_with_warning if target actually moved to the expected landed commit.\n- Update default profiles/instructions to direct and repo-committed summaries; remove direct,recorded default usage.\n- Add regression tests that simulate stale target/conflict/push failure/post-push reporting failure and assert the agent checkout is unchanged and receipt is unambiguous.\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- caco agent reintegrate on a clean direct candidate can land/squash without mutating the agent checkout before publish.\n- If main has moved, reintegrate rejects with stale_base/target_moved and leaves the agent checkout byte-for-byte/HEAD-identical, with a backup ref printed.\n- If a conflict occurs in the isolated integration worktree, reintegrate rejects cleanly and leaves the agent checkout untouched.\n- If a publish succeeds but a later bookkeeping/state/reporting step fails, output says accepted_with_warning or accepted_state_pending and identifies the moved ref/commit; it must not look like a plain failure.\n- Direct-mode summaries can be committed in-repo and land with code; no cacophony-state side effect is required for direct mode.\n- direct,recorded is no longer used as a default in checked-in profiles.\n- Tests cover no-checkout-mutation, backup-ref presence, stale-main rejection, and post-publish verification behavior.\n\nValidation:\n- Run targeted Rust tests for the changed crates.\n- Run cargo test-small or the profile's fast gate if appropriate.\n- Manually exercise a dry-run or fixture reintegration scenario if available.\n\nDo not implement destructive cleanup, force-push agent branches, or remove preserved evidence from bd-95cda5/bd-b8470c while working this.
